d q divides n.\n\nBut p= q +1 must be prime, so p must be a prime number.\n\nSo, in other words, n must be divisible by each prime p where p= q +1 for some q dividing n and p= q +1 is prime.\n\nTherefore, for q= p-1 primes, if q divides n, then p= q+1 must divide n. Therefore, n must be divisible by p= q+1 whenever q divides n.\n\nTherefore, n must be divisible by primes p= q +1 for each q dividing n where p is prime.\n\nTherefore, the necessary condition is that n must be divisible by p= q +1 for each divisor q of n where q+1 is prime.\n\nTherefore, n must be divisible by all primes corresponding to q\'s (divisors of n which are primes minus one) +1.\n\nTherefore, n must be divisible by primes that are one more than the divisors of n that are primes minus one.\n\nSo, to find the minimal n, we need to consider the smallest n that is divisible by all such primes p = q +1 where q divides n and p is prime.\n\nBut how?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model this recursively. Start with n=1, and incrementally build n by multiplying with primes required to satisfy the conditions.\n\nStarting with n=1:\n\nCheck n=1: trivially true.\n\nNext, check n=2: p=2: 2 divides n=1? No. Fine.\n\nBut n=2 is not valid due to previous conditions. But maybe the first non-trivial number is higher.\n\nAlternatively, start from the smallest possible n. Let me try to list possible q\'s and their corresponding p\'s:\n\nq=2 (p=3): if 2 divides n, then 3 divides n.\n\nq=4 (p=5): if 4 divides n, then 5 divides n.\n\nq=6 (p=7): if 6 divides n, then 7 divides n.\n\nq=10 (p=11): if 10 divides n, then 11 divides n.\n\nq=12 (p=13): if 12 divides n, then 13 divides n.\n\nBut q\'s can be composite. For example, q=10: composite, but even if q is composite, p=11 must divide n if q=10 divides n.\n\nWait, but q=10 divides n; does q=10 dividing n imply p=11 divides n? If q=10 divides n, then n must be a multiple of 10, so n=10k. Then, for p=11 to divide n=10k, 11 must divide 10k. But 11 and 10 are coprime, so 